      'The month before the wedding becomes battleground over Jane\'s identity and independence. Rochester wants to take Jane shopping, buy her expensive clothes, deck her in jewels, transform her from plain governess into ornament worthy of his wealth. He\'s excited, possessive, treating her like treasure he\'s won rather than equal partner. Jane is deeply uncomfortable with this. She doesn\'t want expensive things, doesn\'t want to be transformed, doesn\'t want to become his doll or possession. When he takes her to silk warehouses and jewelers, she refuses almost everything—she\'ll accept simple dresses but not satins and jewels, she\'ll be his wife but not his decorated prize. Rochester is frustrated by her resistance but also somewhat amused. He calls her elf, fairy, changeling—diminutive names that reveal he sees her as creature to be possessed rather than equal adult. Jane recognizes the danger: if she lets him transform her, dress her up, make her dependent on his wealth, she\'ll lose herself and become the inferior dependent he could discard.',
      
