    title: 'Next Time: Chapter 25 & 26 — The Interrupted Wedding',
    preview: 'The wedding day arrives. Jane and Rochester proceed to the church, Jane still uneasy but determined to trust him. They stand before the minister, the ceremony begins: "Wilt thou have this woman to thy wedded wife..." The minister asks if anyone knows impediment to this marriage. Silence. The ceremony continues. But just as the minister is about to declare them married, a voice interrupts: "The marriage cannot go on: I declare the existence of an impediment." Mr. Briggs the lawyer steps forward with shocking revelation: Edward Rochester is already married. His wife, Bertha Antoinette Mason, is alive and living at Thornfield—she\'s the figure who tore the veil, the woman locked in the third story, the madwoman in the attic. Rochester admits it\'s true but argues the marriage doesn\'t count: Bertha is violently insane, their marriage was arranged by families, he\'s been trapped for fifteen years. He takes everyone to Thornfield\'s third story to see Bertha. Jane is devastated: the man she loves is already married, their wedding was attempted bigamy, her entire future is destroyed. Rochester begs Jane to stay with him anyway—forget marriage, just be together, who cares about legality. But Jane knows she must leave: staying without marriage would make her mistress, compromise her principles, destroy her self-respect. That night, in agony, Jane makes her decision: she will leave Thornfield, leave Rochester, leave everything she loves to preserve her integrity and independence.',
